1
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Notes on Blindness is a drama, documentary film directed by James Spinney.

2
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:36,000
After losing sight in 1983, John Hull began keeping an audio diary, a unique testimony of
loss, rebirth and renewal, excavating the interior world of blindness.

3
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:21,000
Following on from the Emmy Award-winning short film of the same name, Notes on Blindness
is an ambitious and groundbreaking work, both affecting and innovative.

4
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:06,000
Himself (voice), played by John M. Hull.

5
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:51,000
Herself (voice), played by Marilyn Hull.

6
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:36,000
John Hull, played by Dan Renton Skinner.

7
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:21,000
Marilyn Hull, played by Simone Kirby.

8
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:06,000
Madge Hull, played by Eileen Davies.

9
00:33:45,000 --> 00:33:51,000
Jack Hull, played by David Hobbs.

10
00:37:30,000 --> 00:37:36,000
Notes on Blindness is a 2016 British documentary film
directed by Peter Middleton and James Spinney.

11
00:41:15,000 --> 00:41:21,000
The film profiles writer and theologian John M.

12
00:45:00,000 --> 00:45:06,000
Hull, who became totally blind, just days before the birth of his first
son, after decades of steadily deteriorating vision.

13
00:48:45,000 --> 00:48:51,000
To help him make sense of the upheaval in his life, Hull began documenting his experiences on audio
cassette and wrote his autobiography Touching the Rock: An Experience of Blindness in 1990.

14
00:52:30,000 --> 00:52:36,000
He discusses difficulties such as remembering his families faces, and being unable to find audio versions of the
academic books he wants to read, and thus having to ask friends and family to record them for him.

15
00:56:15,000 --> 00:56:21,000
The film is based upon this work, and his taped diaries and letters.

16
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:06,000
The directors previously created a 2014 Emmy winning short film
on the same subject, which this film builds upon.

17
01:03:45,000 --> 01:03:51,000
The film won the British Independent Film Award for Best Documentary and received nominations for Best Director,
Breakthrough Producer, Best Achievement in Craft (Joakim Sundström for sound) and the Douglas Hickox Award.

18
01:07:30,000 --> 01:07:36,000
At the 70th British Academy Film Awards, the film was nominated for Best British Film, Best
Documentary and Outstanding Debut by a British Writer, Director or Producer.

19
01:11:15,000 --> 01:11:21,000
It was pitched at Sheffield Doc/Fest's 2012 MeetMarket.

20
01:15:00,000 --> 01:15:06,000
Dan Renton Skinner and Simone Kirby depict John Hull and
Marilyn Hull respectively in the film.

21
01:18:45,000 --> 01:18:51,000
"Notes on Blindness: Into Darkness", an immersive VR experience,
released alongside the film, won a XR Peabody Award in 2021.

22
01:22:30,000 --> 01:22:36,000
The film explores themes of biography, reenactment, blindness, 1980s.

23
01:26:15,000 --> 01:26:21,000
The tone is emotional.
